# Git & commits — fieldops-ui

This repository is the UI library only. Commit messages, scopes, and examples refer to library concerns — never to a consuming app’s screens or domain modules.

## Format — Conventional Commits

`type(scope): summary` — imperative mood, no trailing period, ≤72 character summary line.

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `docs`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`, `style`, `perf`.

Scope = layer or component concern, for example:
- `feat(button): add loading state`
- `feat(integrations): add className merge adapter`
- `refactor(core): extract badge status tone map`
- `docs(readme): add install steps`
- `docs(decisions): record Select long-list strategy`

Body (optional, blank line after summary) explains **why**, not what. When the commit encodes a lasting trade-off, point at `docs/decisions.md` — logging law lives only in `030-decisions.mdc` (do not restate the four sections here).

Breaking public API changes use `!` and must be intentional:
`feat(button)!: rename onTap to onPress`

## Granularity

- One logical change per commit. A commit should be revertable on its own without breaking the build.
- Sequence work the way a real library is built: scaffold → tokens/core → integrations → component types → implementation → styles wiring → tests → docs.
- Config, dependency, and lockfile changes get their own `chore:` / `build:` commit, separate from the feature that consumes them — especially when adding a third-party package that will live behind `core/integrations`.
- Never leave `wip` or `fixes` as the final message of a finished unit of work — reword before considering it done.
- Formatting-only changes are their own `style:` commit; do not mix format churn with behaviour changes.
- No secrets, `.env` files, or machine-local ports.

## History hygiene

- Prefer small, reviewable commits that show dependency isolation (integration adapter landed before components call it).
- After a shared or reviewed push, do not rewrite history. Local rebase before the final handoff is fine for a solo submission.
